cured amount will be to make the\\ntrade All right I want to clear up a\\nlot of things People are confused about\\nthis let\'s jump right in All\\nright everyone and Lee Loel here from\\nsmart optionsceller.com Right in front\\nof you you will see the margin rules for\\nselling put options Now for those of you\\nthat don\'t even know what selling put\\noptions are it\'s when you sell a put\\noption contract as your initial\\ntransaction You\'re not buying it and\\nyou\'re not owning it prior You\'re\\nselling it you\'re creating it out of\\nthin air You\'re selling a put option\\nwhich means you\'re obligating yourself\\nto potentially buy shares of a stock\\nsometime down the road at a certain\\nprice which is called the strike price\\nAnd for your obligation you\'re going to\\nget paid money right up front to do that\\nAnd we\'ll look at some examples and I\'ll\\nshow you what that exactly means But for\\nthose of you that are selling put\\noptions that or who want to sell put\\noptions you may not even know what kind\\nof account you\'re already using to sell\\nthose put options what we\'re going to\\ndo is here I\'m going to go over some\\nquick little rules here then we\'ll look\\nat the examples When you have a\\nbrokerage account whether you\'re you\\nknow buying stocks selling stocks\\ntrading options whatever it\'s either\\ngoing to be what\'s called a cash account\\nor a margin account Two different types\\nMost people start off with a cash\\naccount And and on the application when\\nyou\'re filling out the broker\\napplication to open the account you\'ll\\ncome to a section that says \\"Do you want\\nto add margin trading features to this\\naccount?\\" A lot of people don\'t even\\nknow what that means but in this case we\\nalways use in in our newsletter and I\\npersonally always use a margin account\\nAnd if you have a cash account right now\\nyou can always change it to a margin\\naccount You have to fill out\\nanother form at your broker But the two\\ntypes are either a cash account or a\\nmargin account And when you sell a put\\noption contract you do have to put up\\nsome money to make that trade Just\\nif you bought a stock you have to\\npay for that stock When you sell a put\\noption since the bottom line might be\\nthat you might end up buying shares of\\nstock your broker is going to require\\nyou to have some skin in the game and\\nhave some money on hold while the trade\\nis active Now if you have a cash account\\nit\'s going to be what\'s called a cash\\nsecured put option You are going to sell\\na cash secured put option or when you\\nsell a put option you will have what\'s\\ncalled a margin requirement there\'s\\ntwo different things If in if you\'re\\nusing a cash account you will have\\nwhat\'s called selling cash secured put\\noptions If you have a margin account\\nnumber two you will have what\'s called a\\nmargin requirement And we will go over\\nthat And and these two amounts of\\nmoney are completely different Okay And\\nthe one thing that people get confused\\nabout is when they have either the\\nmargin requirement or the cash secured\\nmoney is never taken out of your account\\nwhen you buy shares of stock you\\nhave to pay for those shares of stock Or\\nwhen you buy options you have to pay for\\nthose options right up front and the\\nmoney gets taken out of your account\\nWhen you sell put options the money\\nnever leaves your account Money gets put\\non hold but it never leaves your account\\nright here the money always stays in\\nyour account And the big one here is\\nthat no interest is ever paid ever when\\nyou\'re selling put options You are not\\nborrowing money from anyone When you\\nsell a put option you\'re using the cash\\nthat\'s already in your account to cover\\nthe collateral to sell that put option\\nyou\'re not borrowing any money to do\\nthis Okay People get confused about that\\nAnd I I\'ll jump down to the bottom line\\nhere which when you sell put options\\nthis is not buying on margin Because the\\nword margin is is thrown around here\\nSome people will get confused What\\nbuying on margin is is if you want to\\nbuy shares of stock if you want to buy\\nshares of stock on margin you will put\\nup 50% of the cost to buy those shares\\nof stock and then you will borrow the\\nother 50% of the cost from your broker\\nand they will charge you interest on\\nthat loan that they\'re they\'re giving\\nyou to buy the other half of the the\\nshares of stock That\'s called buying on\\nmargin That is not what\'s happening when\\nyou sell put options When you sell put\\noptions it\'s either going to be a cash\\nsecured amount or what\'s called a margin\\nrequirement Money that\'s already in your\\naccount that\'s going to be held aside\\nwhile the trade is active Okay let\'s\\nquickly let\'s jump right into an\\nexample here and I\'ll show you what how\\nthat is Now let\'s quickly look at a\\nstock chart here Now I pulled up a stock\\nchart of Palunteer A lot of people might\\nknow Palanteer It\'s got a lot of\\nactivity of late Very popular stock It\\nit it it\'s in the cyber security space\\nfor for governments around the world And\\nthe stock\'s been going up up up\\nAlltime new highs here right You know\\nunder $140 a share let\'s assume\\nthat you\'re bullish on Palunteer and you\\nwant to potentially buy shares of\\nPalanteer way down below where it\'s\\ncurrently trading And you\'ve designated\\nI\'m going to throw out a number\\nhere This is not a live recommendation\\nThis is not investment advice This is\\npurely an example let\'s say\\nyou Palanteer and you want to get\\npaid money to put an obligation to buy\\nshares potentially at $75 a share which\\nis all the way down here Okay here\'s the\\n75 level Palunteer\'s trading all the way\\nup here yes when you sell a put\\noption someone will pay you cash for\\nyour obligation Now in this case your\\nobligation is to buy 100 shares at $75 a\\nshare while it\'s currently trading at a\\n