ow if you have a cash account\\nit\'s going to be what\'s called a cash\\nsecured put option You are going to sell\\na cash secured put option or when you\\nsell a put option you will have what\'s\\ncalled a margin requirement And we will go over\\nthat And and these two amounts of\\nmoney are completely different Okay And\\nthe one thing that people get confused\\nabout is when they have either the\\nmargin requirement or the cash secured\\nmoney is never taken out of your account\\nwhen you buy shares of stock you\\nhave to pay for those shares of stock Or\\nwhen you buy options you have to pay for\\nthose options right up front and the\\nmoney gets taken out of your account\\nWhen you sell put options the money\\nnever leaves your account Money gets put\\non hold but it never leaves your account\\nright here the money always stays in\\nyour account And the big one here is\\nthat no interest is ever paid ever when\\nyou\'re selling put options You are not\\nborrowing money from anyone When you\\nsell a put option you\'re using the cash\\nthat\'s already in your account to cover\\nthe collateral to sell that put option\\nyou\'re not borrowing any money to do\\nthis Okay People get confused about that\\nAnd I I\'ll jump down to the bottom line\\nhere which when you sell put options\\nthis is not buying on margin Because the\\nword margin is is thrown around here\\nSome people will get confused What\\nbuying on margin is is if you want to\\nbuy shares of stock if you want to buy\\nshares of stock on margin you will put\\nup 50% of the cost to buy those shares\\nof stock and then you will borrow the\\nother 50% of the cost from your broker\\nand they will charge you interest on\\nthat loan that they\'re they\'re giving\\nyou to buy the other half of the the\\nshares of stock That\'s called buying on\\nmargin That is not what\'s happening when\\nyou sell put options When you sell put\\noptions it\'s either going to be a cash\\nsecured amount or what\'s called a margin\