 up\\nsome money or have some money in your\\naccount that\'s going to be off limits\\nheld aside while this trade is active\\nLet\'s go to the the option chain Okay so\\nhere\'s my broker platform This is an\\noption chain I got Palunteer pulled up\\nThis is a symbol PLTR put options on the\\nright hand side here Bid ask comms\\nstrike right here we\'re going to\\nassume that we want to sell a $75 put\\noption and we we\'re choosing\\nSeptember the September expiration date\\nwhich is 90 days from today Now I make\\nthese these videos on the weekend so\\nsome of these numbers might be a little\\nbit off from what you see during market\\nhours and some of the margin margin\\nrequirement numbers may be a little off\\nas well since the market is not open But\\nI\'m still going to show you everything\\nyou\'re keying in on Palunteer It\'s at\\n137.50 last price in the aftermarket\\nhours You want to sell the 75 puts\\nCollect some cash And then you want to\\nsee how much money is going to be\\nrequired for you to make that\\ntransaction You sell the 75 put Here\'s\\nhow it went out 96 bid at 106 offer at\\nthe end of closing let\'s assume\\nyou can sell this thing for a dollar per\\ncontract Right in the middle somewhere\\nThat means if you sell this thing for a\\ndollar per contract you\'re going to get\\n$100 in your pocket right up front for\\nselling that put option And now you have\\nthe obligation to buy 100 shares at $75\\na share until September 19th 2025 the\\nonly way you\'d have to fulfill your\\nobligation is if Palanteer fell all the\\nway down to 75 by the expiration date If\\nit never falls that far you\'ll keep\\nthat that $100 that you\'re trying to get\\nand you move on to the next trade But so\\nlet\'s assume you\'re going to so\\nyou\'re going to sell this thing Now the\\nway you invoke that in in this trader\\nplatform is that you click on the bid\\nprice Okay let me move myself over here\\na little bit Click on the bid price 96\\nAnd it\'s going to bring up a a line here\\nan order line and you\'re going to sell\\nthis thing And we want to sell it for $1\\nper contract When you click on transmit\\nit\'s going to bring up another window\\nthat shows you what\'s actually going to\\nhappen how much money you need to\\nactually sell this thing Now don\'t worry\\nabout this This is this is what\'s called\\na fake money account paper trading\\naccount you don\'t have to worry about\\nthese these warning messages in here\\nthis is the the box that comes up\\nbefore you actually enter the trade\\nyou\'re going to sell we want to sell\\none contract of this Palenter 75 put And\\nhere\'s how it works And you\'re going to\\nget try to get a $100 for it And on the\\nright side of this this screen here it\\ntells you what\'s going to happen after\\nthe trade or if the trade executes and\\nhow much money Now in this fake money\\naccount right now there\'s over $1.1\\nmillion in it Okay And you see there\'s 0\\nright here There\'s no other positions in\\nthis account Okay When you sell a put\\noption if you have a margin account like\\nthis account is it\'s going to tell you\\nhow much money you have to hold aside in\\norder to sell this $75 put option\\ncontract before the trade occurs you\\nhave $1.1 million in the account And\\nthere\'s no margin requirement whatsoever\\nNow if you make the trade this is what\\nhappens what\'s called post trade So\\nafter the the trade is over you\'re going\\nto get your account value is going to go\\nup by almost $100 because if you\'re\\nsuccessful selling this thing you\'re\\ngoing to get $100 put right into your\\naccount you can see here\'s your\\nbeginning balance here\'s your ending\\nbalance and it\'s going up by almost $100\\nless some commissions But here\'s the\\nnumbers that you want to concentrate on\\nWhat\'s called the initial margin in\\norder to sell the $75 put option you\\nknow the endgame may be you might have\\nto come up with $7,500\\nto buy those 100 shares Now you don\'t\\nknow if you\'re going to end up buying\\nthe shares yet because you don\'t know if\\nPalanteer is going to fall to 75 yet But\\nin this case the broker still needs you\\nto have some skin in the game they\'re\\ngoing to ask you to hold some money\\naside Out of that 1.1 million they\'re\\ngoing to ask you to hold aside $2,529\\nThat\'s off limits now you\'ll have\\nless $1.1 million but you cannot use\\n$2500 of it Okay That\'s going to be off\\nlimits And the broker\'s going to make\\nsure there\'s $2,500 2529 that is off\\nlimits I mean you still have a big chunk\\nhere But for smaller accounts we\'ll talk\\nabout those numbers after you execute\\nthe trade there\'s $2,529\\nthat\'s off limits out of that potential\\n$7,500 that you may have to come up with\\nat the end if Palanteer does fall that\\nfar Okay let\'s talk about what that\\nnumber is how much money that actually\\nis if you have to come up with $7,500\\nin the end if it comes to fruition $2529\\ndivided by 7500 is roughly 33.7%\\nyour broker is only asking you to\\nhold aside 33.7%\\nof that full $7,500 that you may have to\\ncome up with at the end Okay You don\'t\\nknow if you\'re going to buy the shares\\nYou don\'t know if you\'re going to have\\nto come up with the full $7500 but in\\nthe meantime your broker\'s telling you\\nyou have to initially come up with\\n$2,529\\nOkay we\'re going to close this\\nbecause we\'re not going to execute this\\ntrade that\'s roughly 33% that the\\nbroker is requiring to put up or I\\nshould say hold aside out of your free\\ncash Now when the markets when the\\nmarket is live during the week that\\nmargin requirement might be a little\\ndifferent because I know I use the same\\nbroker for my live trading and they\\ntypically hold anywhere from 10% to 20%\\ndepending on how volatile the stock is\\nwhen I sell put options this might be\\na little different on the weekends Now\\nif you were to sell this $75 put option\\nin a cash secured account the broker is\\ngoing to require you to put up or at\\nleast hold aside the full $7500 out of\\nyour balance to make that trade\\nNow this is the reason why I don\'t like\\nto use cash secured accounts because I\\nnever know I have no idea if I\'m\\ngoing to end up buying the shares at $75 a\\nshare why would I want to put up the\\nfull $7,500 if I have no idea if I\'m\\never going to end up buying shares of\\nstock To me that\'s not an efficient use\\nof the money that\'s why I to\\ntrade with a margin account Even though\\nI know I may have to come up with $7,500\\ndown the road if Palunteer falls that\\nfar at least I don\'t have to put up that\\nmuch money to begin with and I can use\\nthat excess cash for other trades\\nLet\'s assume you have a $10,000\\naccount and you\'re using it in a cash\\naccount which means that you have to\\nsell cash secured puts out of your\\n$10,000 you\'re already going to be\\nholding aside $7,500 which means you\\nonly have $2,500 to play with until this\\nother trade comes to fruition for the\\nnext 90 days you\'re holding $7,500\\naside and you don\'t even know if you\'re\\ngoing to buy the shares yet that\'s\\nwhy I to to use a margin account\\nbecause it takes up less money Now\\nremember this money is still intact in\\nyour account It is not being taken away\\nfrom you You still have your full\\n$10,000 in your account It\'s that\\n$7500 of that $10,000 is off limits\\nBut even though it\'s still in your\\naccount same with the margin requirement\\nIf you have $10,000 and the margin\\nrequirements $2,500 now you\\nstill have $7,500 to play with\\nthere\'s a big difference there Let\'s\\ngo back to the let\'s go back to the\\nthe word doc here All right you want\\nto make sure that everyone understands\\nthis Cash secured right here requires\\nthe full amount of the cost to buy the\\nshares whereas the margin requirement\\nneeds a fraction of that cost typically\\nabout 20% Now the 20% is what I see\\nduring trading hours as I said o